# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings, diff drivers and language reporting.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The author works on Windows with core.autocrlf=true; contributors may not.
# Without a .gitattributes, whether a file lands in a commit as LF or CRLF
# depends on whoever happened to touch it, on which machine, with which editor.
# One flip of that would produce a diff touching all 101 tracked .cs files and
# bury the real change. Pinning it here makes the stored bytes the same for
# everyone, whatever their platform or their local git config: attributes beat
# core.autocrlf, so nobody has to configure anything.
#
# THE STATE OF THIS REPOSITORY WHEN THIS FILE WAS ADDED
# Measured, not assumed:
#
#   * The index — what git actually stores — is ALREADY uniformly LF.
#     `git ls-files --eol` reports i/lf for all 111 tracked text files and
#     i/-text for the 3 tracked images.
#   * The WORKING TREE is mixed: 38 files checked out CRLF, 72 LF, and one
#     genuinely mixed file (src/Core/Il2CppDelegates.cs, 5 CRLF endings among
#     its 68 lines).
#
# So this file changes what your working copy looks like, not what is committed.
#
# THE ONE-TIME NORMALIZE STEP
# Adding a .gitattributes does NOT rewrite blobs that are already committed.
# Git only applies the new rules the next time a file passes through the index.
# The command that forces every tracked file through it at once is:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git status                     # inspect BEFORE committing
#     git commit -m "Normalize line endings to LF; add .gitattributes"
#
# That is a whole-repo pass, and on most repositories it produces a single
# large whitespace-only commit that must be landed on its own, never mixed with
# a code change, and never while a branch or PR is open against it.
#
# HERE, IT IS EXPECTED TO STAGE NOTHING. Because the index is already LF, the
# renormalize pass recomputes the identical blob for every one of the 111 text
# files (checked file by file: LF-normalizing each working-tree file reproduces
# its exact index hash). Run it anyway — it is the cheap way to prove the claim
# rather than trust this comment — but `git status` coming back clean is the
# correct outcome, not a sign that something failed.
#
# The 38 CRLF working-tree files are not rewritten by any of this. A fresh clone
# writes them out as LF because of the eol=lf below. An existing working copy
# converts a given file only when git next writes that file for itself — an
# explicit `git checkout -- <path>`, or a branch switch that actually changes
# it; a switch that leaves the file untouched leaves its CRLF untouched too.
# Failing that, on the file's next save, if your editor honours the repository's
# .editorconfig, which sets end_of_line = lf for the same files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: let git detect text, and store AND check out text as LF.
# The `eol=lf` half is what stops Windows checkouts reintroducing CRLF.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicit rules for the file types actually tracked here, so the behaviour
# does not depend on git's text/binary heuristic guessing right.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cs        text eol=lf
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.props     text eol=lf
*.targets   text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.xml       text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf
LICENSE     text eol=lf
NOTICE      text eol=lf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-only scripts: CRLF, deliberately.
# cmd.exe mis-parses a LF-only .bat in ways that are hard to diagnose (a label
# or a trailing `goto` can silently not be found), and PowerShell's own tooling
# assumes CRLF. BADAPPLE_TEST.bat is the only file this currently matches; the
# *.ps1 rule is here so the first PowerShell script added does not have to
# rediscover the problem.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary: never EOL-converted, never diffed as text.
# -text disables all newline conversion; -diff stops git printing a megabyte of
# mojibake when one of these changes. Most of these extensions live under the
# gitignored libs/ and ressources/ trees rather than in the repo, and the rules
# are here so that an accidental `git add -f` of one still behaves sanely.
# Actually tracked today: docs/img/logo.png, docs/img/badapple-thumb.jpg,
# docs/img/mod-ingame.webp.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       -text -diff !eol
*.jpg       -text -diff !eol
*.jpeg      -text -diff !eol
*.gif       -text -diff !eol
*.webp      -text -diff !eol
*.ico       -text -diff !eol
*.gz        -text -diff !eol
*.wav       -text -diff !eol
*.mp3       -text -diff !eol
*.ttf       -text -diff !eol
*.ttc       -text -diff !eol
*.otf       -text -diff !eol
*.dll       -text -diff !eol
*.exe       -text -diff !eol
*.pdb       -text -diff !eol
*.zip       -text -diff !eol
*.7z        -text -diff !eol
*.rar       -text -diff !eol


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Diff drivers. These are built into git — no configuration needed on the
# contributor's side. `diff=csharp` teaches git which line to print in a hunk
# header, so a diff says which method you are looking at instead of showing a
# bare brace. That matters more than usual in this codebase: a typical file
# opens with a long comment block, and roughly one line in five of src/ is a
# comment, so an unlabelled hunk is often indistinguishable from any other.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cs        diff=csharp
*.py        diff=python
*.md        diff=markdown
*.csproj    diff=html
*.props     diff=html
*.targets   diff=html


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub Linguist — what the language bar on the repository page reports.
#
# The intent is honesty, not flattery:
#   * docs/ is documentation and the images in it are illustrations, not source.
#     Excluding it keeps a screenshot and a logo out of the language statistics.
#   * tools/ is NOT excluded. bake_badapple.py, chatbox_probe.py and
#     pick_charset.py are real, working source: they bake the embedded Bad Apple
#     frame data and pick its character ramp, which is how ressources/*.frames
#     is produced in the first place. Marking them vendored or generated would
#     be a lie, so linguist-vendored is explicitly turned off on them in case a
#     future heuristic decides a directory called "tools" is boilerplate.
#   * *.csproj is XML. Linguist already classifies it that way; the line pins it
#     so the classification cannot drift.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/**             linguist-documentation
docs/img/**         linguist-documentation
tools/**            linguist-vendored=false
tools/*.py          linguist-detectable=true
*.csproj            linguist-language=XML
